steps.body:
To make an amount with the fewest coins, grabbing the biggest coin first can fail: with coins 1, 3, and 4, it makes 6 as 4 + 1 + 1, but 3 + 3 uses only 2 coins. A table tries every coin for every amount instead. Let `dp[a]` be the fewest coins that make `a`. The last coin you add is some `c`, which leaves `a - c` to make in the fewest coins, so:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	coins := []int{1, 3, 4}
	amount := 6
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(dp[amount])
}
```

```go
dp := make([]int, amount+1) // dp[0] is 0: no coins make 0
for a := 1; a <= amount; a++ {
	dp[a] = amount + 1 // more than any real answer: not possible yet
	for _, c := range coins {
		if c <= a && dp[a-c]+1 < dp[a] {
			dp[a] = dp[a-c] + 1
		}
	}
}
```

The amounts are filled from 1 up, so `dp[a-c]` is always ready. `amount + 1` stands for "impossible": no answer can be that big, since even all 1s use only `amount` coins. If it's still there at the end, no mix of coins works. Don't use `math.MaxInt64` for this: `dp[a-c]+1` would wrap around to a negative number and win every comparison.

One table answers every amount up to the biggest one you build it for.

