steps.body:
A robot starts in the top-left cell of a grid and moves only right or down. How many paths reach the bottom-right cell? The last move into cell `(r, c)` came from above or from the left, so its count is the sum of those two. That needs a 2D table, `dp[r][c]`, with a row for every grid row:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	grid := []string{"...", ".#.", "..."}
	rows, cols := len(grid), len(grid[0])
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(dp[rows-1][cols-1])
}
```

```go
dp := make([][]int, rows)
for r := range dp {
	dp[r] = make([]int, cols) // each row needs its own make
}
for r := 0; r < rows; r++ {
	for c := 0; c < cols; c++ {
		if grid[r][c] == '#' {
			continue // a wall: no path goes through, it stays 0
		}
		if r == 0 && c == 0 {
			dp[r][c] = 1 // the start
			continue
		}
		if r > 0 {
			dp[r][c] += dp[r-1][c] // from above
		}
		if c > 0 {
			dp[r][c] += dp[r][c-1] // from the left
		}
	}
}
```

Filling row by row, left to right, means the cell above and the cell to the left are always done. The `r > 0` and `c > 0` checks keep the first row and column from reading outside the table. Here the wall in the middle leaves 2 paths, around either side.

The same table answers "the cheapest path" too: store a cost instead of a count, and take the smaller neighbor instead of the sum.

